.TH BOSWAS-REMINDERS 1 "November 2026" "boswas-apps 3.0~alpha1" "Boswas OS"
.SH NAME
boswas-reminders \- show alarms, timers and calendar reminders while the applications are closed
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B boswas-reminders
.RB [ \-\-verbose ]
.br
.B boswas-reminders
.B \-\-status
.br
.B boswas-reminders
.BR \-\-version " | " \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B boswas-reminders
is a small program without a window that runs in the desktop session. It
reads the alarms, timers and focus session of
.BR boswas\-clock (1)
and the events of
.BR boswas\-calendar (1)
from their files, sleeps until the next one is due, and then shows a
desktop notification through org.freedesktop.Notifications:
.IP \(bu 2
an alarm, with the buttons
.B Snooze
and
.BR Dismiss ,
urgent, so that it stays until it is answered;
.IP \(bu 2
a timer that ran down, with
.B Dismiss
and
.BR Restart ;
.IP \(bu 2
the end of a focus period or of a break;
.IP \(bu 2
a reminder of a calendar event, with
.BR Open ,
.B Snooze
and
.BR Dismiss .
Events of calendars that are hidden in Calendar do not remind.
.PP
With an alarm, a timer or a reminder it plays a short chime when
python3\-pyside6.qtmultimedia is installed and there is an audio output.
The chime is played by a helper process, so the service itself never loads
the multimedia libraries.
.PP
The service wakes up when something is due, when one of the files changes,
when the computer resumes from sleep (logind's PrepareForSleep signal), and
otherwise once a minute. Each time it works from the clocks as they are
then, so a wall clock or time zone that was changed neither loses an alarm
nor repeats one. Alarms follow the local time: an alarm set for 07:00 rings
at 07:00 before and after a change to or from summer time; a time in the
hour that is skipped rings one hour later, a time in the hour that
repeats rings once.
.PP
What became due while the computer was off or asleep is handled when the
service runs again: an alarm or timer that is at most ten minutes late and a
reminder of an event that has not ended are shown as usual; everything
older is summed up in one notification per application. Nothing is shown
twice: what was shown is recorded. A snooze is written to disk at once and
survives a restart, and an alarm that was ringing unanswered when the
service stopped rings again when it starts.
.PP
One service runs per session; a second one exits at once with status 0.
The service ends cleanly on SIGTERM, SIGINT and SIGHUP.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-status
Print whether the service runs, the time zone in use, the next alarm and
the next time the service would wake up, and exit with 0 when a service is
running and 1 when none is.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Write more about failed notifications to standard error.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a summary of the options and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/xdg/autostart/com.boswas.Reminders.desktop
Starts the service with the Boswas session (OnlyShowIn=Boswas). To switch
it off for one user, copy the file to
.I ~/.config/autostart
and add the line
.BR Hidden=true .
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/com.boswas.Clock/clock.json
Read; changed (under the same lock Clock uses) when
.B Restart
is pressed on a timer's notification and when an alarm that rings once has
rung.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/com.boswas.Calendar/calendar.json
Read.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/com.boswas.Clock/settings.json
Read for the time format and whether to play the chime.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/com.boswas.Reminders/state.json
What was shown, what is snoozed, and up to when everything was handled.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/boswas-reminders.lock
Locked by the running service.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 after a clean end, or when another service already runs; 1 from
.B \-\-status
when no service runs; 2 for wrong options; 4 when started as root, which
the service refuses.
.SH NOTES
The service needs the session's D\-Bus and a notification service that
supports actions. While there is no notification service it keeps a due
item and tries again for ten minutes. It holds about 45 MB of memory, most
of it shared libraries of Python and Qt, and uses no processor time
between its wake\-ups.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR boswas\-clock (1),
.BR boswas\-calendar (1)
